From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] The activity manager owns the screen, so ask it

0072's own row in patches/VERIFICATION.md asked that "the activity manager and
the window manager agree again" after the hold is let go of. On the device
(2026-11-03, eng.builde.20261103.043516, a userdebug image because this needs
root) they did not.

What was run, and why it took a userdebug image: five attempts on the release
image never reached the branch where the application being waited for goes away
while the hold is in force, because a warm WeRead draws within a few hundred
milliseconds of the deadline and `am force-stop` always landed after the
release. With root the race goes away -- SIGSTOP the process the moment the hold
is taken and it *cannot* draw. That part worked exactly as designed, and it is
the first time this branch has been watched for longer than a few seconds:

    +5s / +20s / +45s with the process frozen   isOnScreen=true
    mHeldOverClosingApps={launcher3/.Launcher}  [1]
    mHeldOverOpeningApps={weread/.LauncherActivity}  [1]
    no "letting go" line

Then the frozen process was force-stopped, and:

    PW3: letting go of 1 held screen(s): an application drew
    launcher3: mViewVisibility=0x8  isReadyForDisplay()=false  isOnScreen=false
    on screen: NavigationBar, StatusBar, ImageWallpaper -- and nothing else
    mResumedActivity: ActivityRecord{... com.android.launcher3/.Launcher}

The screen was the two bars over the wallpaper, which is the defect this whole
branch exists to remove, arrived at from the other side. It does not recover by
itself: an explicit `am start` of the launcher reports "its current task has
been brought to the front" and issues no visibility change. Opening another
application and coming back does fix it, because that is a real transition.

Two causes, and both of them are the same mistake -- reading a flag this file
itself forces instead of asking what the activity manager intends.

  * `hasSomethingOnScreen()` reads `allDrawn`, and `handleClosingApps()` sets
    `allDrawn = true` on every closing application, unconditionally, with
    AOSP's own comment saying why ("regardless of whether it's gotten drawn").
    Stopping the application being waited for therefore makes it look drawn.
    Now it also asks `!hiddenRequested`: an application on its way out does not
    have a picture on the screen, whatever the flag says.
  * The release hid a held screen that the activity manager had already made
    visible again. That is the stuck screen. Now a held token whose
    `hiddenRequested` is false is let go of without being hidden -- the same
    rule 0072 already applied to one that had gone away, for the same reason.

And one more, found by enumerating the states rather than by the device: a hold
whose screens are all gone or all wanted-visible is a hold over nothing, so the
records are dropped instead of waiting for an application that may never
resolve. Without it the dummy animation would sit on a visible window until the
next transition.

The log line said "an application drew" on the strength of the forced flag; it
now says "an application has a picture on screen", which is what the predicate
actually asks.

This is the fifth patch on this branch, and the fourth to be found by running
it rather than by reading it. The states it separates, written out because every
previous attempt died on one that was not: an application that has not drawn; one
that has gone away; one that became the screen being held; one that drew; and
one that was stopped while being waited for, which the forced flag made
indistinguishable from the fourth.

Not run on the device yet when this patch was written.

--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/WindowSurfacePlacer.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/WindowSurfacePlacer.java
@@ -595,8 +595,23 @@
         return true;
     }
 
+    /* Has this application got a picture on the screen?
+     *
+     * allDrawn alone does not answer it, because handleClosingApps() below
+     * forces it on for every closing application -- "Force the allDrawn flag,
+     * because we want to start this guy's animations regardless of whether
+     * it's gotten drawn", AOSP's own line. So an application that was being
+     * waited for and is then stopped reads allDrawn=true without ever having
+     * drawn, and the hold is released as "an application drew". Measured on
+     * the device, 2026-11-03: WeRead's process SIGSTOPped so it could not
+     * draw, then force-stopped; the log said "an application drew" and the
+     * home screen underneath was hidden.
+     *
+     * hiddenRequested is the activity manager's intent, which is the right
+     * authority for "should this be on the screen": an application on its way
+     * out has it true, one that is opening has it false. */
     private static boolean hasSomethingOnScreen(AppWindowToken wtoken) {
-        return wtoken.allDrawn || wtoken.startingDisplayed;
+        return !wtoken.hiddenRequested && (wtoken.allDrawn || wtoken.startingDisplayed);
     }
 
     /* Has this application gone away, as opposed to not having drawn yet?
@@ -630,6 +645,31 @@
         if (mService.mHeldOverClosingApps.isEmpty()) {
             return;
         }
+        // Before asking about the applications being waited for: is there
+        // anything left to hold? A screen that has gone away, or that the
+        // activity manager has made visible again, is not being held by this
+        // any more. If none of them is, the records are stale and keeping them
+        // would leave the dummy animation on a visible window until the next
+        // transition.
+        boolean holding = false;
+        for (int i = mService.mHeldOverClosingApps.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
+            final AppWindowToken wtoken = mService.mHeldOverClosingApps.valueAt(i);
+            if (!isGone(wtoken) && wtoken.hiddenRequested) {
+                holding = true;
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+        if (!holding) {
+            Slog.i(TAG, "PW3: nothing left to hold; dropping "
+                    + mService.mHeldOverClosingApps.size() + " record(s)");
+            for (int i = mService.mHeldOverClosingApps.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
+                mService.mHeldOverClosingApps.valueAt(i).mAppAnimator.setNullAnimation();
+            }
+            mService.mHeldOverClosingApps.clear();
+            mService.mHeldOverOpeningApps.clear();
+            return;
+        }
+
         // Every application that was opening is in one of three states: it has
         // something on the screen, it has gone away, or it is still coming. The
         // hold ends when none of them is still coming -- not when all of them
